WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.305
Runs on the hardware you already have.

00:00:02.685 --> 00:00:03.542
Runs every model.

00:00:03.542 --> 00:00:06.114
And now, for the first time, a major version.

00:00:06.114 --> 00:00:09.542
So what does the one in front of the dot actually promise?

00:00:12.502 --> 00:00:13.404
Hi, I'm Wexley.

00:00:13.404 --> 00:00:16.411
I diffed the last zero release against one point oh.

00:00:16.411 --> 00:00:18.817
Here is where the promise starts and stops.

00:00:20.392 --> 00:00:24.027
For years, llama.cpp had no versions, only build numbers.

00:00:24.027 --> 00:00:28.873
This release was cut from nightly twelve thousand three hundred and fifty-nine.

00:00:29.273 --> 00:00:31.267
Semantic versions started in August.

00:00:31.873 --> 00:00:33.662
Eleven more tags in three months.

00:00:33.662 --> 00:00:37.239
Under zero point x, any of them was allowed to break anything.

00:00:37.639 --> 00:00:39.536
One point oh changes the rule.

00:00:39.536 --> 00:00:43.330
A breaking change to the headers in include now bumps the major,

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:45.227
and the library's soname follows it.

00:00:46.908 --> 00:00:51.085
Callers pass this struct by value, so its layout is compiled into every program.

00:00:51.085 --> 00:00:53.173
Here it is from byte eighty on.

00:00:53.593 --> 00:00:55.667
One point oh deletes a deprecated float.

00:00:55.667 --> 00:00:59.223
But a pointer has to start on a multiple of eight, so

00:00:59.223 --> 00:01:01.297
the gap becomes padding and nothing moves.

00:01:01.717 --> 00:01:03.605
The new field is what breaks it.

00:01:03.605 --> 00:01:06.842
A float at byte one hundred and twenty, and every field after

00:01:06.842 --> 00:01:07.921
it slides eight bytes.

00:01:08.341 --> 00:01:12.008
A binary built against the old header would hand its callback to

00:01:12.008 --> 00:01:13.230
the wrong field, silently.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.369
That is what the soname bump catches.

00:01:17.242 --> 00:01:18.147
Now the file.

00:01:18.147 --> 00:01:21.765
A GGUF starts with a magic word, a version, and two counts.

00:01:22.145 --> 00:01:26.083
Then metadata, then one record per tensor with its type and an offset.

00:01:26.083 --> 00:01:29.718
The data is stored exactly as the kernels read it, so loading

00:01:29.718 --> 00:01:30.930
is a memory map.

00:01:32.392 --> 00:01:37.007
I parsed one real file by range request: a hundred and forty-seven tensors.

00:01:37.427 --> 00:01:40.722
Sorted by type, Q4_K_M turns out to be a mix.

00:01:40.722 --> 00:01:44.346
The embedding and half the down projections stay at six bits.

00:01:45.983 --> 00:01:49.449
Here is a file from 2024 in the same repository.

00:01:49.449 --> 00:01:52.568
As a container, it is perfectly valid version three.

00:01:52.988 --> 00:01:56.589
But most of its tensors use a type that was removed, and

00:01:56.589 --> 00:01:58.690
the reader stops at the first one.

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:00.791
One point oh promises nothing about GGUF.

00:02:02.092 --> 00:02:05.916
I built both releases on a three-oh-ninety and ran the same files.

00:02:05.916 --> 00:02:09.422
Decode speed did not move: every gap is inside the noise.

00:02:11.092 --> 00:02:13.780
The quant matters more than the release.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:18.388
Smaller files decode faster, until the codebook formats: the smallest file here

00:02:18.388 --> 00:02:19.924
is slower than Q4_0.

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:24.326
One point oh binds the C headers and the soname.

00:02:24.326 --> 00:02:27.599
Your GGUF files are on their own: keep the source checkpoint.

00:02:29.775 --> 00:02:33.527
The headers are promised, the struct moved for a new field, and

00:02:33.527 --> 00:02:34.777
GGUF has no promise.

00:02:34.777 --> 00:02:35.715
Thanks for watching!

00:02:36.135 --> 00:02:38.297
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:38.297 --> 00:02:38.915
I'm Wexley.

00:02:38.915 --> 00:02:39.224
Bye!
